---
import EditorialPage from '../components/EditorialPage.astro';

const page = {
  path: '/seo-audit-services/',
  title: 'SEO Audit Services Philippines | Bright Forge Site Audits',
  description: 'An SEO audit from our Philippines team: a full crawl, Search Console review and a prioritised list of findings with evidence, owners and tests for each fix.',
  h1: 'SEO Audit Services in the Philippines',
  crumb: 'SEO audit',
  lead: [
    'An audit tells you why a website is not performing in search and what to do about it, in order. We crawl the whole site, study your Search Console and analytics data, review your content and links, and deliver a list of findings your developer, writer and marketing team can act on.',
  ],
  sections: [
    {
      h2: 'When an audit is worth having',
      html: `<ul class="bf-list">
<li>Traffic has fallen and nobody knows why.</li>
<li>Rankings are stuck despite ongoing work.</li>
<li>You are about to redesign, change CMS or move domain. See <a href="/website-migration-services/">website migration services</a>.</li>
<li>You are about to spend on content or links and want to know it will pay.</li>
<li>A previous agency's work needs checking.</li>
</ul>`,
    },
    {
      h2: 'What we check',
      html: `<dl class="bf-deliverables">
<dt>Crawling and indexing</dt><dd>Can Google reach and index the right pages, and only those.</dd>
<dt>Structure</dt><dd>Site structure and internal links.</dd>
<dt>Technical health</dt><dd>Speed, mobile layout, redirects, canonicals, structured data, JavaScript.</dd>
<dt>Content</dt><dd>Does each important page match what searchers want, and do pages compete with each other.</dd>
<dt>Keywords</dt><dd>Where you are visible, and where competitors are and you are not.</dd>
<dt>Links</dt><dd>Who links to you, and any risk.</dd>
<dt>Local signals</dt><dd>Where relevant.</dd>
<dt>The enquiry route</dt><dd>Forms, phone links and tracking.</dd>
</dl>`,
    },
    {
      h2: 'What you receive',
      html: `<dl class="bf-deliverables">
<dt>A summary for decision makers</dt><dd>A short account of what is wrong and what it is costing.</dd>
<dt>A findings register</dt><dd>Each finding has the affected URLs, the evidence, the recommended change, who should make it, how to test that it worked, and a priority: fix first, fix next, plan, or ignore.</dd>
<dt>A 90-day roadmap</dt><dd>The findings in the order we would tackle them.</dd>
<dt>A handover call</dt><dd>With the people who will do the work.</dd>
</dl>`,
    },
    {
      h2: 'How we do it, step by step',
      html: `<ol class="bf-steps">
<li>You grant read-only access to Search Console and analytics, and tell us the decision the audit must inform.</li>
<li>We agree which markets, templates and sections are in scope, and quote.</li>
<li>Crawl and technical diagnosis.</li>
<li>Content, keyword and competitor review.</li>
<li>Link and risk review.</li>
<li>Write-up, senior review and handover.</li>
</ol>`,
    },
    {
      h2: 'Why a software report is not an audit',
      html: `<p>Audit tools list hundreds of warnings and give them equal weight. Most do not matter. The value of an audit is in the judgement: which few problems are holding the site back, and which can be left alone. We wrote about this in <a href="/blog/why-most-seo-audits-miss-the-real-problems/">why most SEO audits miss the real problems</a>.</p>`,
    },
    {
      h2: 'Timeframe and pricing',
      html: `<p>Most audits for small to mid-sized businesses take one to two weeks, depending on site size, data access and complexity. Larger ecommerce, marketplace or multi-location sites may need longer.</p>
<p>We do not publish a rate card. Each audit is quoted in a written proposal, and the scope depends on the size of the site and the number of markets. An audit is also the usual first step of our <a href="/seo-services-philippines/">monthly SEO services</a>.</p>`,
    },
  ],
  faqs: [
    { question: 'Can you fix what you find?', answer: 'Yes, or your own team can. The register is written to be used either way.' },
    { question: "Do you need access to our website's admin?", answer: 'Not for the audit. Read-only Search Console and analytics access is enough.' },
    { question: 'Is this different from a technical SEO audit?', answer: 'A technical audit covers crawling and indexing, structure and technical health. A full audit adds content, keywords, links and the enquiry route. See <a href="/technical-seo-services-philippines/">technical SEO</a> and <a href="/keyword-research-services/">keyword research</a>.' },
  ],
};
---
<EditorialPage page={page} />
